---
import Project from '../../../layouts/Project.astro';
import PageTitle from '../../../components/PageTitle.astro';
import Apparatus from '../../../components/Apparatus.astro';
import CiteBlock from '../../../components/CiteBlock.astro';
import Factsheet from '../../../components/Factsheet.astro';
import hallyu from '../../../data/hallyu.json';
import { projects } from '../../../data/projects';
import { buildDate } from '../../../data/build';

const p = projects.find((x) => x.slug === 'hallyu-indian-press')!;
const rows = [
  { term: 'Sources', html: 'Times of India, Hindustan Times, Indian Express and The Hindu in licensed full text (ProQuest, at the host institution); The Hindu 2000–2010 via the Internet Archive CDX API; GDELT DOC 2.0 for 2017 onward.' },
  { term: 'Pilot corpus', html: `${hallyu.pilot.corpus_docs} full-text documents (${hallyu.pilot.hindu_years}), SQLite with FTS5, cached raw responses and full provenance.` },
  { term: 'Ethics & licensing', html: 'Newspaper text is copyrighted: this project publishes metadata, counts and derived statistics only. Harvesting is polite, identified and rate-limited, with no paywall or robots bypass. Code MIT, derived data CC BY 4.0.' },
  { term: 'Stage', html: 'Source audit complete. Corpus collection phased and gated; framing analysis not begun.' },
  { term: 'Build', html: `Figures generated from verified counts searched 18 August 2026; site built ${buildDate}.` },
];
---
<Project title="Looking for Hallyu in the Indian press — Method and data"
  description="Sources, coverage windows, pipeline, ethics, limits and citation for the Indian press corpus project.">
  <PageTitle title="Method and data"
    lede="Which archives were searched, what each one can and cannot support, how the pipeline works, and what will be published." />
  <section class="band band--flush">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="small muted breadcrumb"><a href={p.href}>← {p.shortTitle}</a></p>

      <section class="section first">
        <h2>The source audit</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            The audit is the finished part of this project, and it is reported as a result rather than as
            preparation. Each finding below was established by live probing rather than from documentation.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><b>GDELT DOC 2.0 has a hard floor at 2017-01-01.</b> Earlier queries are rejected outright, and what it returns is URL and title metadata, not full text. It cannot serve 2000–2016 at all.</li>
            <li><b>Licensed full text begins in different years for each paper</b> at the host institution: Hindustan Times 2005, Times of India 2006, Indian Express 2009, The Hindu 2015. No single window covers the whole period.</li>
            <li><b>From 2017 the databases index online-only editions</b> alongside print. Any count that mixes the two rises for reasons that have nothing to do with publishing.</li>
            <li><b>Internet Archive preservation of The Hindu grows roughly twentyfold across 2000 to 2008,</b> so density in the web archive measures archiving, not publishing. Retrieval in the pilot reached 79%; the worst years for missing captures are 2002 and 2003.</li>
            <li><b>The Indian Express archive hosts are dead</b> and the live Hindu archive is bot-gated. Neither was bypassed; the licensed copy is the only legitimate route.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <Apparatus label="Why the audit is published">
          <p>
            These constraints apply to anyone building a corpus from the Indian English-language press, and
            none of them is documented in the databases' own materials. Publishing the audit converts work
            that would otherwise be invisible into something another researcher can rely on or contest.
          </p>
        </Apparatus>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Pipeline</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Three stages: <b>discover</b> enumerates archive discovery pages; <b>harvest</b> fetches
            candidates cache-first at polite rates, with permanent caching so that re-running a stage never
            re-touches the network; <b>build</b> compiles a full-text-searchable corpus in SQLite with FTS5,
            carrying provenance for every document. Vocabulary matching is two-tier: a broad Korea net for
            recall, then the candidate Hallyu vocabulary for precision, evaluated against licensed full-text
            counts rather than assumed.
          </p>
          <p>
            The candidate vocabulary is version 0.9 and is deliberately not frozen: it will be fixed only
            after a retrieval test measures its recall and precision on a stratified sample. Provenance
            (staff, wire, unknown) is a core variable rather than a nuisance field, because wire share differs
            sharply between these papers and a wire story is not evidence of a newspaper's framing choices.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>At a glance</h2>
        <div class="measure"><Factsheet rows={rows} /></div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Limits</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <ul>
            <li>No framing analysis has been carried out; nothing here is a claim about how the press framed anything.</li>
            <li>2000–2004 has no licensed full-text source at the host institution and remains unvalidated.</li>
            <li>The pre-2011 absence is validated against licensed full text but still awaits recall validation against a historical database.</li>
            <li>Discovery in the web-archive strand is headline-level, which is a known recall limitation.</li>
            <li>One annual count (Times of India, 2022) is anomalously low against its neighbours and is treated as a suspected database gap rather than as evidence.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2 id="cite">Cite</h2>
        <CiteBlock
          citation={`Bilal, Mohd. Looking for Hallyu in the Indian Press, 2000–2026: a source audit and corpus in development. Digital humanities project, ${p.version}. The Academy of Korean Studies, 2026.`}
          licence="Derived data CC BY 4.0 · code MIT. Newspaper text is not redistributed."
          url="https://mohdbilaldh.github.io/projects/hallyu-indian-press/"
          buildDate={buildDate} />
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>
</Project>
<style>
  .breadcrumb { margin: 0 0 24px; }
  .first { margin-top: 0; }
</style>
